<main>
  <p class="eyebrow">andesim hybrid · vsim:ax46mpv_fpga_l3 · 2026-08-04 → 08-06</p>
  <h1>Running Bonsai 8B and 27B, and what it buys</h1>
  <p class="sub">How the 4B flow was extended to the 8B model (two flags, zero rebuilds)
  and then to the 27B (a guest-RAM lift plus one rv64 kernel fix), and the measured
  speedup of the Q1_0 optimization chain on each model's RTL node.</p>

  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><b class="accent">11.23x</b><span>8B end-to-end, pristine → vd4dots</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b class="accent">11.86x</b><span>27B end-to-end, pristine → vd4dots</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>0.14–0.16 cyc/elem</b><span>final kernel cost across an 8.6x range of node sizes</span></div>
  </div>

  <hr class="rule">
  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">part 1</p>
    <h2>What must change to run the 8B model</h2>
    <p>The 4B recipe carries over unchanged: same four binaries, same engine, same
    ROI marker at K=51. The model file is a runtime argument. Only two flags are
    new, and both exist because the 8B model presses against the 2&nbsp;GiB guest
    RAM window.</p>

    <div class="req">
      <code class="flag">--mem-size 2097148K</code>
      <div class="why"><b>andesim flag.</b> The exact hybrid maximum: 2&nbsp;GiB minus the
      4&nbsp;KiB hybrid scratch area. Hybrid mode rejects <code>2G</code> outright
      (only fast mode accepts it), so this literal value is required.</div>
      <code class="flag">--no-mmap</code>
      <div class="why"><b>llama.cpp flag.</b> On RISC-V the Q1_0 repack buffer type accepts
      these tensors, so with mmap the 1,099&nbsp;MiB file mapping <em>and</em> the
      931&nbsp;MiB repack buffer are both resident at load time. Together with the
      other buffers that exceeds the window. <code>--no-mmap</code> drops the mapping;
      everything else fits with 565&nbsp;MiB to spare.</div>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <p class="fig-title">Guest RAM demand at load, drawn to scale (1&nbsp;MiB = same width everywhere)</p>
      <div class="mem">
        <div style="position:relative; height:18px; min-width:620px">
          <span class="winlabel" style="position:absolute; left:calc(79.3% - 120px)">2,048&nbsp;MiB window&nbsp;↓</span>
        </div>
        <div class="memrow">
          <p class="memname">default (mmap) — total demand 2,582 MiB — <span class="over">over the window by 534 MiB, load fails</span></p>
          <div class="membar">
            <div class="seg map"   style="left:0%;      width:42.56%">file mapping 1,099</div>
            <div class="seg repack" style="left:42.56%; width:36.06%">repack buffer 931</div>
            <div class="seg other" style="left:78.62%;  width:21.38%">other 552</div>
            <div class="winline" style="left:79.3%"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="memrow">
          <p class="memname">with <code>--no-mmap</code> — total 1,483 MiB — <span class="fit">fits, 565 MiB headroom</span></p>
          <div class="membar">
            <div class="seg repack" style="left:0%;     width:36.06%; border-radius:4px 0 0 4px">repack buffer 931</div>
            <div class="seg other" style="left:36.06%;  width:21.38%">other 552</div>
            <div class="winline" style="left:79.3%"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>“Other 552” = non-repacked weights 168 + KV cache 72 (at -c 512) +
      compute graph 312. The hatched zone past the dashed line is outside the
      2,048&nbsp;MiB window. Removing the file mapping is the whole fix: the repacked
      copy is the one the kernels read, so the mapping was pure duplication.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>The full command (the two 8B flags highlighted):</p>
    <pre><code>cd ~/work/andesim
TMPDIR=~/tmp ./andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger marker --runtime linux \
  <span class="hl">--mem-size 2097148K</span> --engine vsim:ax46mpv_fpga_l3 --step-timeout-ms 14400000 \
  ~/work/llama.cpp/bin-d4 -- \
  -m ~/work/llama.cpp/models/Bonsai-8B-Q1_0.gguf \
  <span class="hl">--no-mmap</span> -no-cnv --prompt 'The capital of France is' -n 32 -c 512 -t 1</code></pre>

    <details>
      <summary>Why the binaries need no rebuild</summary>
      <div class="inner">
        <p>The ROI marker counts MUL_MAT nodes, not shapes. Bonsai-8B is the same
        qwen3 architecture as 4B — 36 layers, 253 MUL_MAT per token — so compiled-in
        K=51 lands on the same node: the layer-7 V projection. Only its shape grows,
        2560×1024 → 4096×1024. Each run self-reports the node, so this is checked,
        not assumed:</p>
        <pre><code>[roi] MUL_MAT #51 src0=blk.7.attn_v.weight [4096 x 1024] nrows=2</code></pre>
        <p>The repack gate also passes untouched: 1024 output rows, and 1024 mod 64 = 0.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule">
  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">part 2</p>
    <h2>What must change to run the 27B model</h2>
    <p>The 27B (3.8&nbsp;GB, qwen35 hybrid attention) needs three lifts beyond the 8B
    recipe. Binaries and engine stay the same.</p>

    <div class="req">
      <code class="flag">guest RAM &gt; 2 GiB</code>
      <div class="why"><b>Runtime lift.</b> The andesim runtime now places guest RAM at the
      high window <code>0x8_00000000</code> (DRAM alias + DRAM_EXT, contiguous) whenever
      <code>--mem-size</code> exceeds the low window — fast and hybrid modes, up to 16G.
      The vsim leg maps a low-2G alias over the same memory so the RTL still fetches the
      low-linked code. At or below 2&nbsp;GiB the historical map is kept, so every recorded
      cycle number stays comparable (re-measured bit-identical).</div>
      <code class="flag">--mem-size 6G --no-mmap</code>
      <div class="why"><b>Same duplication math as 8B, bigger numbers.</b> With mmap, the
      3,891&nbsp;MiB mapping plus the ~3.1&nbsp;GiB repack buffer exceed 6G — and at this
      size an over-budget load grinds rather than failing fast, so the flag is mandatory.
      <code>--no-mmap</code> fits with room to spare.</div>
      <code class="flag">rv64 vec_dot fix</code>
      <div class="why"><b>llama.cpp <code>17b246b44</code>.</b> qwen35's delta-net exercises
      <code>ggml_vec_dot_f32</code>, whose RVV accumulator init merged into an uninitialized
      register; GCC&nbsp;14 could re-materialize it under the wrong vector type, corrupting
      6 of 8 accumulator registers. Fixed with a plain init at vlmax. qwen3 (4B/8B) never
      calls this helper on its decode path, so their results were always correct and stand
      unchanged. After the fix, 27B rv64 output matches the x86 host at temp&nbsp;0.</div>
    </div>

    <p>On qwen35's graph, compiled-in K=51 lands on the layer-6 attention gate —
    self-reported, so the node identity is checked, not assumed:</p>
    <pre><code>[roi] MUL_MAT #51 src0=blk.6.attn_gate.weight [5120 x 6144] nrows=2</code></pre>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule">
  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">part 3</p>
    <h2>Speedup on the 8B and 27B nodes</h2>
    <p>Same optimization chain as 4B, measured per model on its K=51 node
    (8B: <code>attn_v</code> 4096×1024 · 27B: <code>attn_gate</code> 5120×6144, both
    nrows=2, RTL cycles on <code>ax46mpv_fpga_l3</code>). Every stage keeps output
    bit-exact.</p>

    <figure>
      <p class="fig-title">8B — RTL cycles for the K=51 node, linear scale, zero baseline</p>
      <div class="chart" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: pristine 7,278,168 cycles; repack 3,475,147; repack plus prefetch 2,218,540; vd4dots 648,246 which is 11.23 times faster than pristine.">
        <div class="crow">
          <div class="cname">pristine<small>stock ggml</small></div>
          <div class="ctrack"><div class="cbar" tabindex="0" style="width:100%"
            data-tip="stock ggml scalar/vector path · 1.74 cyc/elem · baseline 1.00x">
            <span class="barlabel inside">7,278,168&ensp;·&ensp;1.00x</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="crow">
          <div class="cname">repack<small>row-parallel N=64</small></div>
          <div class="ctrack"><div class="cbar" tabindex="0" style="width:47.75%"
            data-tip="Nx1 row-parallel repack · 0.83 cyc/elem · step 2.09x">
            <span class="barlabel inside">3,475,147&ensp;·&ensp;2.09x</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="crow">
          <div class="cname">+ prefetch<small>DIST=1</small></div>
          <div class="ctrack"><div class="cbar" tabindex="0" style="width:30.48%"
            data-tip="software prefetch on weight stream · 0.53 cyc/elem · step 1.57x">
            <span class="barlabel inside">2,218,540&ensp;·&ensp;3.28x</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="crow">
          <div class="cname">+ vd4dots<small>XAndesVDot, F9</small></div>
          <div class="ctrack"><div class="cbar final" tabindex="0" style="width:8.91%"
            data-tip="nds.vd4dots kernel + one vsetvli per 4 dots · 0.15 cyc/elem · step 3.42x">
            <span class="barlabel outside"><b style="color:var(--copper-deep)">648,246&ensp;·&ensp;11.23x</b></span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="axis"><div></div><div>
          <span style="left:0%">0</span><span style="left:27.5%">2M</span>
          <span style="left:55%">4M</span><span style="left:82.4%">6M</span>
        </div></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>Hover a bar for cycles per element and the per-stage step.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure>
      <p class="fig-title">27B — RTL cycles for the K=51 node, linear scale, zero baseline</p>
      <div class="chart" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: pristine 54,314,202 cycles; repack 25,629,722; repack plus prefetch 16,317,304; vd4dots 4,578,346 which is 11.86 times faster than pristine.">
        <div class="crow">
          <div class="cname">pristine<small>stock ggml</small></div>
          <div class="ctrack"><div class="cbar" tabindex="0" style="width:100%"
            data-tip="stock ggml path · 1.73 cyc/elem · baseline 1.00x">
            <span class="barlabel inside">54,314,202&ensp;·&ensp;1.00x</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="crow">
          <div class="cname">repack<small>row-parallel N=64</small></div>
          <div class="ctrack"><div class="cbar" tabindex="0" style="width:47.19%"
            data-tip="Nx1 row-parallel repack · 0.81 cyc/elem · step 2.12x">
            <span class="barlabel inside">25,629,722&ensp;·&ensp;2.12x</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="crow">
          <div class="cname">+ prefetch<small>DIST=1</small></div>
          <div class="ctrack"><div class="cbar" tabindex="0" style="width:30.04%"
            data-tip="software prefetch on weight stream · 0.52 cyc/elem · step 1.57x">
            <span class="barlabel inside">16,317,304&ensp;·&ensp;3.33x</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="crow">
          <div class="cname">+ vd4dots<small>XAndesVDot, F9</small></div>
          <div class="ctrack"><div class="cbar final" tabindex="0" style="width:8.43%"
            data-tip="nds.vd4dots kernel + one vsetvli per 4 dots · 0.15 cyc/elem · step 3.56x">
            <span class="barlabel outside"><b style="color:var(--copper-deep)">4,578,346&ensp;·&ensp;11.86x</b></span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="axis"><div></div><div>
          <span style="left:0%">0</span><span style="left:27.6%">15M</span>
          <span style="left:55.2%">30M</span><span style="left:82.9%">45M</span>
        </div></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>The two charts use different scales (150k vs 1.1M cycles per unit,
      about the 7.5x node-size ratio) — the bars look alike because the speedups are alike.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>build</th><th>8B cycles</th><th>8B total</th><th>27B cycles</th><th>27B total</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>pristine</td><td>7,278,168</td><td>1.00x</td><td>54,314,202</td><td>1.00x</td></tr>
        <tr><td>repack</td><td>3,475,147</td><td>2.09x</td><td>25,629,722</td><td>2.12x</td></tr>
        <tr><td>+ prefetch</td><td>2,218,540</td><td>3.28x</td><td>16,317,304</td><td>3.33x</td></tr>
        <tr><td>+ vd4dots (F9)</td><td>648,246</td><td><b>11.23x</b></td><td>4,578,346</td><td><b>11.86x</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <p>The chain transfers because the per-element cost barely moves across an 8.6x
    range of node sizes: repack and prefetch are essentially shape-invariant (the
    prefetch stage stays at 0.26 cyc/elem/act on every model), and vd4dots varies
    only a few percent with the streamed bytes per row. The 8B and 27B totals land
    within 0.6x of the 4B reference (12.34x).</p>

    <details>
      <summary>Per-element comparison across models</summary>
      <div class="inner tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>build</th><th>4B cyc/elem</th><th>8B cyc/elem</th><th>27B cyc/elem</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
